{"reference":"James 2","book":"James","chapter":2,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"chapter_overview","title":"Overview","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"James joins two closely connected concerns: favoritism toward the wealthy humiliates the poor, and a claim to faith that supplies no practical help to a person in need is empty. Abraham and Rahab are then presented as examples in which deeds enact or complete faith. The chapter’s argument is therefore not about verbal belief alone, but about faith made visible in concrete action.","verse_refs":["James 2:1-7","James 2:14-17","James 2:21-25"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_james_002_6ae6ec887f8f6a356f273b55"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"cultural_context","title":"Law, Mercy, and Partiality","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"In verses 8–13, love of neighbor is called the “royal law.” James places mercy alongside judgment, so the rejection of favoritism is not an isolated rule: it belongs within the chapter’s concern for how people are treated.","verse_refs":["James 2:8-13"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_james_002_589b0746fc2be14f070e486f"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"fact"}]},{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Questions About Faith and Works","blocks":[{"id":"block_3","text":"James addresses the person who claims faith while withholding material aid or obedient action. Interpreters differ over how “justified” functions in verses 21–25—whether declarative, vindicatory, or demonstrative—and over how this discussion relates to other biblical uses of similar language. The chapter itself presses the inseparability of professed faith and enacted care; it should not be used to portray Jewish law as bondage or to romanticize poverty.","verse_refs":["James 2:1-7","James 2:14-26","James 2:21-25","James 2:8-13"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_james_002_c2ceb303017685efa6fb3803"],"confidence":"low","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_4","text":"Rahab is paired with Abraham as an enacted-faith example. Later Christian connections involving Rahab and “rest” belong to later canonical reception and should be kept separate from what James itself claims here.","verse_refs":["James 2:25"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_james_002_fbf72efe76a19db3b5102d41"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"James Within the Letter","blocks":[{"id":"block_5","text":"Across the letter, James combines an epistolary greeting with sustained moral exhortation, wisdom contrasts, examples, questions, and prophetic warning. Describing it as wisdom, diatribe, sermon, or anthology may highlight real features, but no single label exhausts its form; the rapid, probing argument of chapter 2 fits this wider pattern.","verse_refs":["James 1:1-27","James 3:1-18","James 4:1-17","James 5:1-20"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_james_002_e62f312e24eeb469e0df7933"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}